    "body": "Licensed from Kelun-Biotech, Merck’s TROP2-targeting antibody drug conjugate sac-TMT improved overall survival in advanced gastroesophageal cancer while missing on two secondary endpoints.\nMerck’s TROP2 antibody drug conjugate sac-TMT improved overall survival in a late-stage gastroesophageal cancer trial—the second Phase 3 success in its global program of 18 late-stage studies.\nThe study did miss on two secondary endpoints, however: progression free survival (PFS) and objective response rate (ORR), according to a Friday press release .\nNeither Merck nor Kelun-Biotech, the China startup that discovered sacituzumab tirumotecan (sac-TMT), shared data behind the win.\nThe topline readout comes from TroFuse-015 , which recruited 473 patients with advanced gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma who have received two or more previous lines of systemic therapy. The study met its main goal of significantly improving overall survival (OS) when compared to treatment of a physician’s choice (TPC).\nThe results were generally consistent across subgroups regardless of TROP2 expression status, according to Merck. The Kelun-shared antibody drug conjugate (ADC) is designed to target TROP2 , a cell-surface protein overexpressed in several cancers.\nSac-TMT failed to significantly improve PFS and ORR, though the latter wasn’t formally tested under the prespecified analysis plan, according to Merck.\n“While TroFuse-015 did not meet the secondary endpoint of PFS, and the nominal benefit in ORR was not formally tested, it’s not unheard of in oncology trials to see this dynamic (typically with immunotherapies) and OS remains the gold standard in these settings,” RBC Capital Markets wrote in a Friday morning note to investors.\nSafety findings were consistent with previous studies, with no new safety signals observed. The Big Pharma plans on sharing the data with regulatory authorities and at an upcoming medical conference, according to Merck’s announcement.\nSac-TMT is currently approved in four indications in China and is under FDA priority review for previously treated endometrial cancer, with a decision expected by June 2027. In the U.S., the asset secured a Commissioner’s National Priority Voucher—an FDA pilot program designed to speed up reviews for products that aligned with certain national priorities. However, the program, which was launched under former FDA Commissioner Marty Makary, is now reportedly defunct .\nFDA\nFDA’s commissioner voucher program reportedly shuttered ahead of Senate vote on Overton\nIn advance of a Senate vote on Heidi Overton as the next FDA commissioner, the agency has shelved the Commissioner’s National Priority Voucher program introduced by former leader Marty Makary, Reuters reported Thursday.\nNovember 12, 2026\n·\n3 min read\n·\nHeather McKenzie\nRead more\n“Sac-TMT is the first TROP2 ADC to improve OS in a global Phase 3 study in advanced gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma compared to TPC, demonstrating its potential in a particularly challenging disease setting,” Marjorie Green, senior vice president and head of oncology, global clinical development at Merck Research Laboratories, said in the release. “There is a tremendous unmet need for therapies that can improve survival for these patients.”\nFriday’s results follow the positive October readout for TroFuse-005 , which found that sac-TMT slashed the risk of death by 32% in advanced or recurrent endometrial cancer compared to chemotherapy.\nThe TroFuse program consists of 18 ongoing Phase 3 studies across numerous tumor types, including endometrial, bladder, breast, cervical, gastric and ovarian cancers. Fourteen of those trials are pursuing first-in-class opportunities, including TroFuse-015.\nBeyond the global TroFuse program, sac-TMT is also being tested in lung cancer, succeeding this summer in a late-stage study for first-line PD-L1-positive non-small cell lung cancer.\nThe ADC is situated to serve as a “meaningful part of the Keytruda solution,” BMO analysts wrote in a September report, referring to Merck’s PD-1 blockbuster that faces exclusivity losses at the end of 2028, putting $33 billion in revenue at risk.\nBMO analysts have been bullish on Merck’s revenue-replacement plan and have projected peak sales of $4.3 billion for sac-TMT.\nMerck joined in on development of the asset in May 2022, offering Kelun-Biotech up to $1.36 billion in biobucks to secure certain rights, according to a release translated from Chinese. Later that year, the pharma came back for more from Kelun-Biotech, offering up to $9.3 billion for seven investigational ADCs.\nCancer\nESMO 2026: Merck’s Kelun-partnered ADC cuts death risk by over 30% in endometrial cancer\nSac-TMT significantly extended overall and progression-free survival for patients with advanced or recurrent endometrial cancer by 6.9 months over chemotherapy in a Phase 3 trial—becoming the first TROP2-directed ADC to do so in this setting, Merck told BioSpace.\nOctober 26, 2026\n·\n4 min read\n·\nHeather McKenzie\nRead more\nCorrection (Nov. 20): This story was updated to state that this study looked at multiple types of gastroesophageal cancer, not just stomach cancer. BioSpace regrets the error.",
